    {
      "id": "organizer",
      "title": "Public and private law: use the table as a comparison tool",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "832357bdb0425dcdf43d14b70cf05950b3dc763f7b5d2cec1b17659990787300",
          "sha256": "3c67cab79f25f4ac4766d9413240e88e77195a9bfcb6e30d97d31dc5adc611fb",
          "locator": "Public Law v. Private Law Organizer, comparison table"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The blank organiser asks for general definitions, legal fields, parties, burden of proof, outcomes and punishment. It does not itself supply the answers. Fill it using the class’s worked cases and record which type of proceeding each claim describes; do not invent a single burden of proof applying to every public or private dispute.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "832357bdb0425dcdf43d14b70cf05950b3dc763f7b5d2cec1b17659990787300",
              "sha256": "3c67cab79f25f4ac4766d9413240e88e77195a9bfcb6e30d97d31dc5adc611fb",
              "locator": "Public Law v. Private Law Organizer, comparison table"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The two Rule of Law worksheets support retrieval practice: match causes to effects and then connect constitutional examples to the six safeguards. The graphic organiser is for explaining relationships, rather than memorising the skit dialogue.",
